\lispPackageChapter{chorus/lang}

This is the kernel language. \texttt{chorus} reexports it. Every generated
\texttt{.cu} file includes \texttt{int.h}, \texttt{float.h},
\texttt{float3.h}, \texttt{float4.h}, \texttt{double.h}, \texttt{double3.h},
\texttt{double4.h}, and \texttt{curand.h} from the \texttt{include/}
directory.

\section{Index variables}

\texttt{grid-dim-x}, \texttt{grid-dim-y}, \texttt{grid-dim-z},
\texttt{block-dim-x}, \texttt{block-dim-y}, \texttt{block-dim-z},
\texttt{block-idx-x}, \texttt{block-idx-y}, \texttt{block-idx-z},
\texttt{thread-idx-x}, \texttt{thread-idx-y}, and \texttt{thread-idx-z}.

\section{Statements}

\texttt{set} stores a value. \texttt{with-shared-memory} declares shared
memory for the block.

\section{Types}

Scalar types: \texttt{void}, \texttt{bool}, \texttt{int}, \texttt{float},
\texttt{double}, and \texttt{curand-state-xorwow}.

Vector types, also available as Lisp structures: \texttt{float3},
\texttt{float4}, \texttt{double3}, and \texttt{double4}. Each has a
constructor \texttt{make-float3} (and the same pattern for the others), slot
readers, a predicate, an equality test, and a \texttt{with-float3} macro.

Pointer types: \texttt{bool*}, \texttt{int*}, \texttt{float*},
\texttt{double*}, \texttt{float3*}, \texttt{float4*}, \texttt{double3*},
\texttt{double4*}, and \texttt{curand-state-xorwow*}.

\texttt{cffi-type} maps a Chorus type to the CFFI type used at a launch.

\section{Built-in operations}

\texttt{xor}, \texttt{shl}, \texttt{shr}, \texttt{rsqrt}, \texttt{\_\_exp},
\texttt{\_\_divide}, \texttt{atomic-add}, \texttt{pointer},
\texttt{syncthreads}, \texttt{double-to-int-rn}, \texttt{dot},
\texttt{curand-init-xorwow}, \texttt{curand-uniform-float-xorwow},
\texttt{curand-uniform-double-xorwow}, \texttt{curand-normal-float-xorwow},
and \texttt{curand-normal-double-xorwow}.

\section{Compiler entry points}

The compiler packages export one operation each:
\texttt{compile-symbol}, \texttt{compile-type},
\texttt{type-of-expression}, \texttt{compile-expression},
\texttt{compile-statement}, and \texttt{compile-kernel}.
\texttt{compile-kernel} is what the kernel manager calls.
